# 2026-03-08 — Today I Learned: Object.hasOwn over hasOwnProperty

- Use Object.hasOwn(obj, prop) to check own properties safely and succinctly.
- It's safer than obj.hasOwnProperty(prop) when obj may be null or have a null prototype.

```js
// Preferred (modern, clear)
const obj = Object.create(null);
obj.foo = 1;
console.log(Object.hasOwn(obj, 'foo')); // true
console.log(Object.hasOwn(obj, 'toString')); // false

// Safe polyfill/fallback for older environments
const hasOwn = Object.hasOwn ?? ((o, p) =>
  Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(o, p)
);

// Usage works even if obj has no prototype
console.log(hasOwn(obj, 'foo')); // true
```

- Small takeaway: prefer Object.hasOwn for readability and robustness when checking own properties.